From 89c730e8ab4976f9dd087e0630c18ea5567fa915 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:31:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] parse: a prefix range is an operand of a unary or binary
 operator

rustc 1.90 tests/ui, parse-only run:

    parser/ranges-precedence.rs    let reference = &..0;
                                   let sum = Foo { foo: 3 } + ..4;
                                   expr!(!..0); expr!(-..0); expr!(*..0);
    parser/issues/issue-105634.rs  let _c = &..;
    error: Unexpected token TOK_DOUBLE_DOT

Reference, Range expressions: RangeToExpr `..` Expression,
RangeToInclusiveExpr `..=` Expression and RangeFullExpr `..` are
expressions, so they may stand wherever an operand is expected; the
precedence table only says how far their end reaches. rustc parses the
operand of `!`, `-`, `*` and `&` (parse_expr_prefix_common,
parse_expr_borrow) and every right-hand side of a binary operator
(parse_expr_assoc_with) as a prefix range when it starts with `..`,
`..=` or `...`.

mrustc only took a prefix range where a whole expression starts
(Parse_Expr1_1). Parse_Expr13, the unary level that every operand
position reaches first, now hands a leading `..` / `..=` / `...` to
Parse_Expr1_1, so `&..0` is `&(..0)`, `x + ..4 * 2` is `x + (..(4 * 2))`.

Grammar copies: the macro_rules expression skimmer already took `..`
in operand position (it reads ranges as binary operators with optional
operands); checked with `$e:expr` given `&..9` and `!..0u32`. The
proc-macro visitor and ast/dump print ranges with their operands.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
---

diff --git a/src/parse/expr.cpp b/src/parse/expr.cpp
index 395c854f..84cca586 100644
--- a/src/parse/expr.cpp
+++ b/src/parse/expr.cpp
@@ -1107,6 +1107,13 @@ ExprNodeP Parse_Expr13(TokenStream& lex)
     Token   tok;
     switch(GET_TOK(tok, lex))
     {
+    // A prefix range as the operand of a unary or binary operator (`&..0`, `!..0`, `x + ..4`), as rustc's
+    // parse_expr_prefix_common / parse_expr_assoc_with: its end extends as far as that of a range expression
+    case TOK_DOUBLE_DOT:
+    case TOK_DOUBLE_DOT_EQUAL:
+    case TOK_TRIPLE_DOT:
+        PUTBACK(tok, lex);
+        return Parse_Expr1_1(lex);
     case TOK_DASH:
         return NEWNODE( AST::ExprNode_UniOp, AST::ExprNode_UniOp::NEGATE, Parse_Expr12(lex) );
     case TOK_EXCLAM:
